## 3. Identify the repository you will connect

Open a terminal in your existing React Native repository, where `package.json` is located. Run:

```bash
# Read the current checkout without changing files or remote services.
git status --short --branch
git branch --show-current
git rev-parse HEAD
git remote get-url origin
```

| Command | Read the result |
| :--- | :--- |
| `git status --short --branch` | Shows the branch and local changes. Changed or untracked files will not appear in a remote build until committed and pushed. |
| `git branch --show-current` | Record the source branch. A blank result means a detached checkout; select the intended source branch before preparing enrollment. |
| `git rev-parse HEAD` | Record the exact application commit. Later, compare it with the commit shown in the Bitrise build. |
| `git remote get-url origin` | Identify the remote repository. Keep embedded credentials out of recorded URLs. |

**Expected result:** you can identify the application repository, source branch, and commit. If Git reports “not a git repository,” open the correct checkout and repeat these commands.

Keep the application source branch separate from the configuration repository's review and promotion branches. They identify different changes.

| If the command fails | Next action |
| :--- | :--- |
| No Git repository | Open the actual application checkout. |
| No `origin` remote | Use Repository Preparation to publish/connect the approved private repository. |
| No commit exists | Review and publish the initial application source through the team's process before enrollment. |
| Branch output is blank | Select the intended source branch; do not enroll an unexplained detached checkout. |

## 4. Obtain the access and enrollment details

Use your existing Enterprise access process and support Teams channel. Ask Platform Engineering for the following details for this application:

| Detail | Why you need it |
| :--- | :--- |
| Private source repository access | To publish application code and create PRs. |
| Central configuration repository and request process | To submit the GitOps enrollment YAML. |
| Approved template ID and exact version | To select the governed build configuration. |
| Approved source branch | To identify the application branch the platform will build. |
| Access group and intended QA/UAT audience | To assign the correct platform access and distribution scope. |
| Bitrise workspace access | To inspect the provisioned app, build logs, and artifacts. |
| Sauce Labs and NowSecure access, as required by your role | To inspect device-test evidence and security findings. |

Do not copy example portal addresses, emails, or channel names as real contact details. Use the actual Enterprise contacts.

**Expected result:** you know where to submit the request, which template to select, and who owns provisioning. If a value is unavailable, obtain it from the platform owner before submitting the request.

### Separate your actions from platform operations

| You, the developer | Platform Engineering / authorized owner |
| :--- | :--- |
| Prepare source, native targets, CAMP metadata, and tests. | Provide approved templates, runners, and integration access. |
| Submit the secret-free enrollment request for review. | Perform protected provisioning and bind approved credentials. |
| Fix application failures and inspect delivery evidence. | Manage repository authorization, webhooks, signing material, and platform recovery. |

Under the supplied governed enrollment process, Platform Engineering manages the scanner, Private setting, SSH/HTTPS authorization, webhooks, and credential bindings. Submit the GitOps request rather than manually creating another app.

## 5. Recognize the configuration files

These are the files you will work with across the next pages. Do not create empty placeholders just to match this table.

| File | Repository / location | Purpose |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| `campfile.yml` | Application root | Application identity, environment identifiers, business metadata, and analytics mappings from the supplied CAMP schema. |
| `<app_name>.yaml` | Central configuration repository, approved enrollment directory | Request creation of the app using an approved template and access assignments. |
| `bitrise.yml` | Location required by the approved workflow/template | Defines build automation and triggers. |
| `dangerfile.ts` | Approved review-tooling location | Implements automated PR feedback. |
| `saucelabs.request.yml` | Application root | Supplies metadata consumed by the Enterprise Sauce Labs integration. |
| `.sauce/config.yml` | Application repository | Configures the selected supported device-test runner. |

Approved automation applies the enrollment request to create the platform resource. CAMP describes the application. Neither file contains credentials.

## 6. Understand when delivery is complete

| Status you see | Meaning |
| :--- | :--- |
| PR validation passed | The reviewed revision passed its configured checks. It does not prove live provisioning. |
| Bitrise build passed | The configured build completed. Inspect required test and security results as well. |
| Upload succeeded | The destination received an artifact. Processing, signing, audience assignment, or availability may still be pending. |
| Delivery verified | The intended audience can access the exact expected version at the approved destination. |

The governed routes are `qa_android`, `qa_ios`, `uat_android`, and `uat_ios`. Verify each applicable route independently. Google Play internal testing and TestFlight are store delivery destinations; successful QA delivery does not imply public production release.

**Workshop example:** QA Android delivery was verified; QA iOS built but failed downstream signing; UAT publication was unconfirmed. Record each route separately.

If an external create/upload operation times out with an unknown result, preserve the existing state and ask the platform owner to reconcile it. Repeating the write can create duplicates.

