> **Confidential — Enterprise internal use only.** Keep credentials, binaries, private download links, and sensitive evidence in approved systems. Redact screenshots.

## 1. Select the destination

| Destination | Input | Owner-confirmed prerequisites |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| Private Android QA/UAT | Approved APK | App Management identity, signing policy, destination environment, and audience. |
| Private iOS QA/UAT | Approved IPA | App Management identity, compatible signing/distribution, and audience. |
| Google Play Internal Testing | Signed AAB | Existing store record, package, version code, API service account, track, and tester setup. |
| App Store Connect/TestFlight | Store-compatible IPA | Existing app record, bundle ID, version/build, API connection, and tester setup. |

The four governed private routes remain `qa_android`, `qa_ios`, `uat_android`, and `uat_ios`. Public production release requires its separate authorization and store process. Supplied policy requires TGRC approval before UAT/Production.

Use **App Store & Distribution** for account registration, store records, service-account/API-key provisioning, store forms, review, and public rollout. Those operations remain authorized-owner tasks. The supplied Play step requires the first app upload through the Play web interface before subsequent automated versions; confirm that prerequisite with the store owner. [Google Play Deploy contract](https://github.com/bitrise-steplib/steps-google-play-deploy/blob/master/step.yml).

## 2. Verify the exact handoff file

**Where:** a Bash Script step after the final signing output is available, before destination upload.

```bash
set -euo pipefail

# Documentation-owned binding: select exactly one final candidate file.
: "${MOBILE_DELIVERY_ARTIFACT:?Final candidate path is required}"
test -s "$MOBILE_DELIVERY_ARTIFACT"
shasum -a 256 "$MOBILE_DELIVERY_ARTIFACT"
```

Bind `MOBILE_DELIVERY_ARTIFACT` to the actual signed APK/AAB/IPA from the approved strategy. It is an example-owned name, not an additional SOT output.

Match the file to its application ID, version/build, route, source commit, template, signing evidence, and required gate results. A hash or non-empty file does not verify these properties by itself. Hash again after any approved transformation/re-signing and preserve the relationship between input and output.

